Remanufacturing is the only end-of-life strategy where used products are returned to like-new condition.  From the customer’s perspective, remanufactured products are difficult to perceive and they have the same warranties as new products. Remanufacturing uses higher content of work compared to the refurbishment processes, resulting in superior consistency and efficiency in its goods. Remanufactured goods undergo significant changes, although repaired and refurbished products maintain their identity. This is because all commodity parts are tested through remanufacturing, and those that can not be restored to original performance specifications are replaced with new ones. The newly remanufactured product will then be made up of these mixed parts. Remanufacturing is important because researchers have shown that it is an economically significant industrial activity. Remanufacturing is particularly applicable to high-quality products where the reverse industrial practice makes economical sense. Also, this process is reputed to lead to increased profit and creates a demand for skilled employment.